            {
              "id": "d324f78a-b337-349a-8626-b75fc35bf27b",
              "name": "Exposure of Private Asymmetric RSA Key in Library File",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Exposure of Private Asymmetric RSA Key in Library File\" vulnerability in Docker programming language refers to the insecure storage of a private RSA key within a library file. This private key is used for asymmetric encryption, where the public key is used to encrypt data and the private key is used to decrypt it. If an attacker gains access to this private key, they can decrypt any data that was encrypted with the corresponding public key, leading to a potential data breach.\n\n## Security Concern\n\nThis is a major security concern because the exposure of the private key can lead to unauthorized access to sensitive data. An attacker can use the private key to decrypt sensitive information, impersonate the user, or even perform a Man-in-the-Middle attack. This can lead to data breaches, identity theft, and other serious security incidents.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nThe best way to mitigate this vulnerability is to never store private keys in your source code or any other insecure location. Instead, use secure methods to store and retrieve your keys, such as environment variables, secure key vaults, or configuration files that are not included in the source code repository.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of hardcoding the private key in your source code, you should store it in an environment variable:\n\n```javascript\nconst privateKey = process.env.PRIVATE_KEY;\n```\n\nThen, you can set the `PRIVATE_KEY` environment variable in your production environment. This way, the private key is not exposed in your source code and is only accessible to authorized users in the production environment.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThis code does not appear to have any library dependencies.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-798: Use of Hard-coded Credentials](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
