            {
              "id": "3d4f7601-d5d2-318d-a0df-b4ccc6a52725",
              "name": "Insecure Cryptographic Hash Identified: MD5, SHA1 Inadequate for Security; bcrypt, scrypt, Argon2 Recommended for Passwords, SHA-256+ for Integrity Checks.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nInsecure Cryptographic Hash Identified: MD5, SHA1 Inadequate for Security; bcrypt, scrypt, Argon2 Recommended for Passwords, SHA-256+ for Integrity Checks is a vulnerability that arises when cryptographic hash functions with known weaknesses, such as MD5 and SHA1, are used in security contexts. These hash functions are considered insecure due to their susceptibility to collision attacks, where two different inputs produce the same hash output. This can lead to unauthorized access or data integrity issues.\n\nIn JavaScript, this vulnerability can occur when the `crypto` module's `createHash` method is used with 'md5' as the argument, as shown in the provided code snippet.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use stronger hash functions. For password hashing, consider using bcrypt, scrypt, or Argon2. These algorithms are designed to be computationally intensive, making brute force attacks more difficult. For integrity checks, consider using SHA-256 or higher.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the use of MD5 with a more secure hash function. For example, if you're using the `crypto` module, you can replace 'md5' with 'sha256' or 'sha512'. Here's how you can do it:\n\n```javascript\nconst crypto = require('crypto');\nconst hash = crypto.createHash('sha256');\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `crypto` module, which is a built-in module in Node.js, so no additional dependencies are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Password Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-327",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-327"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ba9bc9ce-d8f3-3c5d-9929-f743d7bebf8e",
              "name": "JWT Token Vulnerability Detected",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the insecure storage of JSON Web Tokens (JWT) in the local storage of a web browser. JWTs are often used to store sensitive information, such as user authentication data. Storing them in local storage can expose them to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks, where an attacker can inject malicious scripts into web pages viewed by other users, to steal their tokens.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to store JWTs in a secure HttpOnly cookie. This prevents the token from being accessed by JavaScript running in the browser, thus protecting it from XSS attacks. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of storing the JWT in local storage, you should set it as a HttpOnly cookie. Here is an example of how to do this using the `express` and `cookie-parser` libraries in Node.js:\n\n```javascript\nconst express = require('express');\nconst cookieParser = require('cookie-parser');\n\nconst app = express();\napp.use(cookieParser());\n\napp.get('/login', (req, res) => {\n  // Authenticate user and generate JWT...\n  const token = 'eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.eyJkYXRhIjp7fX0.bVBhvll6IaeR3aUdoOeyR8YZe2S2DfhGAxTGfd9enLw';\n  \n  res.cookie('token', token, { httpOnly: true });\n  res.send('Logged in!');\n});\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `express`: A web application framework for Node.js.\n- `cookie-parser`: Middleware for parsing cookies in Express apps.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP JWT Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/JSON_Web_Token_Cheat_Sheet_for_Java.html)\n- [CWE-352: Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF)](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/352.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-352",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/352.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-352",
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "89d53b9d-9a39-3528-92bf-1b5b02bba0bf",
              "name": "XSS Vulnerability Risk in User-Controlled Data Methods such as innerHTML, outerHTML, and document.write",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the `dat.utils.css.inject` function is vulnerable to XSS because it uses the `innerHTML` property to add user-controlled data to the page. If an attacker can control the `e` parameter, they can inject arbitrary HTML or JavaScript code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate XSS vulnerabilities, you should:\n\n- Use the principle of least privilege: Only allow necessary permissions for the code that needs to run.\n- Validate input: Ensure that user-supplied data is safe before using it.\n- Escape output: Ensure that user-supplied data is properly escaped before it is included in a web page.\n- Use secure APIs: Use functions that explicitly make it clear that a string is safe and does not need to be escaped.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the use of `innerHTML` with `textContent` or other safer methods. Here is the fixed code:\n\n```javascript\ndat.utils.css=function(){\n    return{\n        load:function(e,a){\n            a=a||document;\n            var b=a.createElement(\"link\");\n            b.type=\"text/css\";\n            b.rel=\"stylesheet\";\n            b.href=e;\n            a.getElementsByTagName(\"head\")[0].appendChild(b)\n        },\n        inject:function(e,a){\n            a=a||document;\n            var b=document.createElement(\"style\");\n            b.type=\"text/css\";\n            b.textContent=e; // Changed from innerHTML to textContent\n            a.getElementsByTagName(\"head\")[0].appendChild(b)\n        }\n    }\n}();\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code does not appear to have any library dependencies.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS (Cross Site Scripting) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f2c2347c-a7d6-31b5-ae95-d439b71af101",
              "name": "Non-Literal Argument Vulnerability in Require Function",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Non-Literal Argument Vulnerability in Require Function\" is a type of vulnerability in JavaScript that occurs when the argument to a `require()` function is not a string literal, but a variable or a function call. This can lead to a potential security risk as it allows an attacker to load and execute arbitrary code or modules in the application.\n\nIn the provided code, the `require()` function is using a non-literal argument to load a module. The argument is a string template that includes a function call to `utils.extractFilename()`. This function extracts a filename from the application's configuration, which could potentially be manipulated by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using non-literal arguments in `require()` function calls. Instead, use string literals to specify the module to be loaded. If dynamic module loading is necessary, ensure that the input is properly validated and sanitized to prevent an attacker from loading arbitrary modules.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nconst filename = utils.extractFilename(config.get('application.chatBot.trainingData'));\nif (allowedFiles.includes(filename)) {\n  const trainingData = require(`data/chatbot/${filename}`);\n}\n```\n\nIn this fix, we first extract the filename and then check if it is in a list of allowed files before loading it with `require()`. This ensures that only expected modules can be loaded.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- A module loader, such as Node.js, to provide the `require()` function.\n- A utility library, represented by `utils`, to provide the `extractFilename()` function.\n- A configuration library, represented by `config`, to provide the `get()` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10 - A1 Injection](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A1_2017-Injection)\n- [CWE-94: Improper Control of Generation of Code ('Code Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-94",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-94"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "25b53782-4070-3c77-9c89-180719a340f6",
              "name": "Potential ReDoS vulnerability due to RegExp() being called with a `d` function argument. Consider using hardcoded regexes, input validation, or a regex checking/sanitization library like 'recheck'.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe code snippet provided is vulnerable to a Regular Expression Denial of Service (ReDoS) attack. This vulnerability occurs when the `RegExp()` function is called with a `d` function argument. An attacker can exploit this vulnerability by providing a specially crafted input that causes the regular expression to take a very long time to evaluate, effectively causing a denial of service.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, consider the following:\n\n1. **Hardcoded Regexes**: Instead of dynamically creating regular expressions, use hardcoded regular expressions whenever possible. This reduces the risk of an attacker being able to control the regular expression.\n\n2. **Input Validation**: Validate inputs before passing them to the `RegExp()` function. This can prevent an attacker from providing a malicious input.\n\n3. **Regex Checking/Sanitization Library**: Use a library like 'recheck' to check and sanitize regular expressions before they are used.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the vulnerable line:\n\n```javascript\nreturn RegExp(\"(?:^|\\\\s+)\"+d+\"(?:\\\\s+|$)\").test(a.className)||!1\n```\n\nWith a safer alternative:\n\n```javascript\nlet safeD = d.replace(/[^a-zA-Z0-9]/g, '');\nreturn RegExp(\"(?:^|\\\\s+)\"+safeD+\"(?:\\\\s+|$)\").test(a.className)||!1\n```\n\nThis code replaces any non-alphanumeric characters in `d` with an empty string, reducing the risk of a ReDoS attack.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code snippet does not appear to require any specific library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP ReDoS](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Regular_expression_Denial_of_Service_-_ReDoS)\n- [CWE-400: Uncontrolled Resource Consumption](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/400.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-400",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/400.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-400"
                ]
              }
