            {
              "id": "0d711d0a-3da9-3f55-b87a-04d4127c0404",
              "name": "\"XSS Vulnerabilities from User-Controlled Data in Methods like `innerHTML`, `outerHTML`, or `document.write`\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the vulnerability arises from the use of `innerHTML` property. This property is used to get or set the HTML content inside an element. If this property is used to insert user-controlled data, it can lead to XSS attacks if the data is not properly sanitized.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate XSS vulnerabilities, you should:\n\n- Use the principle of least privilege. Only necessary privileges should be granted, and they should be granted for the least amount of time necessary.\n- Validate and sanitize user input. All user-controlled data should be considered untrusted and should be validated and sanitized before use.\n- Use safe APIs. Some browser APIs are designed to create HTML or JavaScript and can be used safely. For example, `element.textContent` is safer than `element.innerHTML`.\n- Use Content Security Policy (CSP). CSP is a computer security standard introduced to prevent XSS attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the use of `innerHTML` with `textContent`:\n\n```javascript\nname:{get:function(){return a.name},set:function(b){a.name=b;q&&(q.textContent=a.name)}}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code snippet does not provide enough context to determine the library dependencies.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
