              "id": "3a194dad-3069-3535-9a3d-4f9f96ca4cdf",
              "name": "Prototype Pollution Vulnerability Detected",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nPrototype Pollution is a vulnerability that occurs when the properties of JavaScript \"prototype\" objects are unsafely manipulated. The \"prototype\" object in JavaScript is special because it allows properties to be shared among all instances of an object. If an attacker can control and manipulate these properties, they can potentially affect all objects of the same type, leading to issues like denial of service, property injection, or even remote code execution.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the vulnerability arises from the fact that the `property` can be controlled by an attacker and used to manipulate the `replacementValue` object.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. Avoid directly assigning user-controlled input to object properties.\n2. Use input validation to ensure that incoming data is of the expected type and format.\n3. Use a well-maintained library that provides secure methods for manipulating object properties.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA simple fix for the provided code snippet would be to validate the `property` before using it:\n\n```javascript\nif (typeof property === 'string' && property.indexOf('__proto__') === -1 && property.indexOf('prototype') === -1 && property.indexOf('constructor') === -1) {\n    replacementValue = replacementValue[property];\n}\n```\n\nThis code checks if `property` is a string and if it does not contain potentially dangerous values like `__proto__`, `prototype`, or `constructor`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet does not appear to require any specific library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [CWE-250: Execution with Unnecessary Privileges](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/250.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are subject to change and may not be accessible at a later date."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-250",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/250.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-250"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ed643f60-c552-35eb-98a4-77e2e3e13f77",
              "name": "\"XSS Vulnerabilities from User-Controlled Data in Methods like `innerHTML`, `outerHTML`, or `document.write`\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn JavaScript, methods like `innerHTML`, `outerHTML`, or `document.write` can introduce XSS vulnerabilities if they are used to insert user-controlled data into the DOM. These methods interpret the inserted data as HTML, including any scripts that it may contain.\n\nIn the provided code example, `snarkdown(hint.text)` is used to convert Markdown to HTML, and the result is inserted into the DOM using `textBox.innerHTML`. If `hint.text` contains user-controlled data, this could introduce an XSS vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate XSS vulnerabilities from user-controlled data in methods like `innerHTML`, `outerHTML`, or `document.write`, you should:\n\n- Use text content instead of HTML when inserting user-controlled data into the DOM. In JavaScript, you can use the `textContent` property instead of `innerHTML` or `outerHTML`.\n- If you need to insert HTML into the DOM, sanitize the HTML first to remove any potentially harmful scripts. There are libraries available that can do this, such as DOMPurify.\n- Use Content Security Policy (CSP) to restrict the sources from which scripts can be loaded. This can prevent the execution of injected scripts.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the provided code example:\n\n```javascript\ntextBox.textContent = snarkdown(hint.text);\n```\n\nIn this version, `textContent` is used instead of `innerHTML`, so the result of `snarkdown(hint.text)` is inserted as text, not HTML. This prevents any scripts in `hint.text` from being executed.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code example requires the following library:\n\n- snarkdown: A small Markdown parser.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
