              "id": "77e90292-e808-34df-8330-f325aed66345",
              "name": "\"Potential XXE Attack due to Use of parseXml() Function with `noent` Field Set to `true`\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe code snippet provided is vulnerable to XML External Entity (XXE) attacks due to the use of the `parseXml()` function with the `noent` field set to `true`. This configuration allows the parsing of XML entities, which can be exploited by an attacker to read local files, interact with internal systems, or execute a denial-of-service attack.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should disable the parsing of XML entities. This can be achieved by setting the `noent` field to `false` when calling the `parseXml()` function. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```javascript\nconst xmlDoc = vm.runInContext('libxml.parseXml(data, { noblanks: true, noent: false, nocdata: true })', sandbox, { timeout: 2000 })\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- `vm`: This is a core Node.js module used to compile and run code within V8 Virtual Machine contexts.\n- `libxml`: This is a library used for parsing XML and HTML documents. It's not a core Node.js module and needs to be installed separately.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XXE Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/XML_External_Entity_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-611: Improper Restriction of XML External Entity Reference ('XXE')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/611.html)"
              },
