            {
              "id": "a2b015e1-477b-377c-bce5-e2fd5772d83b",
              "name": "\"Cryptographic Weakness in crypto.pseudoRandomBytes()/Math.random() Random Number Generator\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `Math.random()` function in JavaScript is not cryptographically strong. It is a pseudo-random number generator with known weaknesses. The `crypto.pseudoRandomBytes()` function in Node.js is also not cryptographically strong. It is a pseudo-random number generator that uses the OpenSSL RAND_bytes function, which is not suitable for generating cryptographic keys or other sensitive data.\n\nThe use of these functions can lead to predictable values and can be exploited by an attacker to guess the generated values, leading to a variety of attacks such as session prediction, brute force attacks, etc.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use a cryptographically strong random number generator. In Node.js, you can use the `crypto.randomBytes()` function, which generates cryptographically strong pseudo-random data. The byte array can then be converted to a number if needed.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the use of `Math.random()` with `crypto.randomBytes()`. Here is how you can do it:\n\n```javascript\nconst crypto = require('crypto');\n\nlet quantity = product.quantity ?? parseInt(crypto.randomBytes(4).toString('hex'), 16) % 70 + 30;\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `crypto` library, which is a built-in module in Node.js, so no additional installation is needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak Pseudo-Random Number Generator (PRNG)](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-338",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-338"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f7575d5c-5051-3e0a-8e13-a9ed3dafe491",
              "name": "\"Crypto.pseudoRandomBytes()/Math.random() exhibits cryptographic weakness.\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `Math.random()` function in JavaScript is not suitable for generating cryptographic secrets. The values produced by `Math.random()` are predictable enough to be guessed and exploited by an attacker, which can lead to various security vulnerabilities such as information disclosure, session prediction, and other forms of attacks that rely on knowing the pseudo-randomly generated values.\n\nThe `crypto.pseudoRandomBytes()` function in Node.js is also not suitable for generating cryptographic secrets. It generates pseudo-random data, which is not cryptographically strong. This means that the generated data can be predictable and can be exploited by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nUse `crypto.randomBytes()` instead of `crypto.pseudoRandomBytes()` or `Math.random()`. The `crypto.randomBytes()` function generates cryptographically strong pseudo-random data that can be used for generating secrets.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is the fixed version of the code:\n\n```javascript\nconst crypto = require('crypto');\n\nfor (let i = 0; i < length; i++) {\n    text += possible.charAt(crypto.randomBytes(1)[0] % possible.length);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `crypto` library, which is a built-in module in Node.js.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Node.js Security Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-330: Use of Insufficiently Random Values](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/330.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-330",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/330.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-330"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "07e79d94-7129-36e2-87fe-697bae2dd4a8",
              "name": "\"Hardcoded Username Detected: Secure in Environment Variable\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Hardcoded Username Detected: Secure in Environment Variable\" is a vulnerability that occurs when a username is hardcoded into a JavaScript application. This is a security risk because it exposes sensitive information that could be exploited by an attacker. If an attacker gains access to the source code, they can easily identify the hardcoded username and potentially use it to gain unauthorized access to the system.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid hardcoding sensitive information such as usernames, passwords, or other secrets directly into your code. Instead, use environment variables to store this information. Environment variables are a type of dynamic-named value that can affect the way running processes behave on a computer. They are part of the environment in which a process runs.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of hardcoding the username into your code, you should store it in an environment variable. Here's how you can do it:\n\n```javascript\n// Old vulnerable code\n// memory.user = 'john';\n\n// Recommended fix\nmemory.user = process.env.USERNAME;\n```\n\nIn the above code, `process.env.USERNAME` is an environment variable that stores the username. You can set the value of this environment variable in your system or in your deployment configuration.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example provided does not require any specific library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10 - A3:2017-Sensitive Data Exposure](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A3_2017-Sensitive_Data_Exposure)\n- [CWE-798: Use of Hard-coded Credentials](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-798",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-798"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "73349733-bc99-356f-b941-b2c5c405c1cf",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA potential Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability can occur when user-controlled data is used in an HTML string. This can allow an attacker to inject malicious scripts into web pages viewed by other users. In the provided JavaScript code, the error message is being logged using a template literal that includes a function call to `utils.getErrorMessage(err)`. If the `err` object is user-controlled and not properly sanitized, it could lead to an XSS vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to properly sanitize any user-controlled data before using it in an HTML string. This can be done by using a library that provides HTML encoding functions, or by manually replacing potentially dangerous characters with their HTML-encoded equivalents.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```javascript\nconst sanitize = require('sanitize-html');\n\nlogger.error(`Could not create quantity: ${sanitize(utils.getErrorMessage(err))}`);\n```\n\nIn this fix, the `sanitize-html` library is used to sanitize the error message before it is logged. This will prevent any potentially dangerous characters in the error message from being interpreted as HTML or JavaScript.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n- `sanitize-html`: This library is used to sanitize the error message before it is logged.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7e7c36d9-b8fa-3630-9c64-1138b828bb3c",
              "name": "Path Traversal Vulnerability due to Inadequate Input Validation and Path Canonicalization in Application Process",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nPath Traversal Vulnerability due to Inadequate Input Validation and Path Canonicalization in Application Process is a common vulnerability in JavaScript programming language. It occurs when an application uses user-controllable data to construct a pathname that is intended to identify a file or directory located underneath a restricted parent directory, but the data is not properly validated or sanitized, allowing for directory traversal characters to be injected.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the application is using the user-supplied `file` variable to construct a path to a file that is then sent to the client. If the `file` variable is not properly validated, an attacker could potentially manipulate the path to access files outside of the intended directory.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. Avoid using user-supplied input to construct file paths.\n2. If it is necessary to use user-supplied input, validate the input thoroughly. This includes checking for path traversal characters and sequences (`../` or `..\\`).\n3. Use a library or framework function that generates the absolute path and validates it to be under the expected base directory.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```javascript\nvar path = require('path');\nvar express = require('express');\nvar app = express();\n\napp.get('/download', function(req, res){\n  var file = req.params.file;\n  var filePath = path.join(__dirname, 'ftp', file);\n  var resolvedPath = path.resolve(filePath);\n\n  if (resolvedPath.startsWith(path.join(__dirname, 'ftp')) && fs.existsSync(resolvedPath)) {\n    res.sendFile(resolvedPath);\n  } else {\n    res.status(404).send('File not found');\n  }\n});\n```\n\nIn this fix, the `path.join()` function is used to construct the file path, and `path.resolve()` is used to generate the absolute path. The `startsWith()` function is then used to ensure that the resolved path is under the intended directory.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `path`: a core Node.js module for handling and transforming file paths.\n- `express`: a minimal and flexible Node.js web application framework.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Path Traversal](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Path_Traversal)\n- [CWE-22: Improper Limitation of a Pathname to a Restricted Directory ('Path Traversal')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/22.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-22",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/22.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-22"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "3f994158-b57d-3074-a97c-16bf4538fafb",
              "name": "Path Traversal Vulnerability due to Inadequate Input Validation and Path Canonicalization",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nPath Traversal Vulnerability due to Inadequate Input Validation and Path Canonicalization is a common vulnerability in web applications. It occurs when an application uses user-supplied input to construct a file path for file operations. If the user input is not properly validated, an attacker can use special characters (like \"../\") to traverse up to parent directories and access sensitive files outside of the intended directory. In JavaScript, this vulnerability can be exploited when using methods like `res.sendFile()` or `path.resolve()` without proper input validation and path canonicalization.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. Avoid using user-supplied input to construct file paths directly.\n2. If it's necessary to use user input, validate the input strictly. For example, only allow alphanumeric characters, and reject special characters or sequences like \"../\".\n3. Use path canonicalization to resolve the file path to its simplest form, ensuring it's within the intended directory.\n4. Use a built-in or third-party library to safely handle file paths.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nvar express = require('express');\nvar path = require('path');\nvar app = express();\n\napp.get('/download/:file(*)', function(req, res) {\n  var file = req.params.file;\n  var fileLocation = path.join(__dirname, 'encryptionkeys', file);\n  res.download(fileLocation, file);\n});\n```\n\nIn this code, `path.join()` is used to safely construct the file path. It also uses `res.download()` instead of `res.sendFile()`, which automatically handles path traversal issues.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Express.js: A web application framework for Node.js.\n- Path: A built-in Node.js module for handling and transforming file paths.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Path Traversal](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Path_Traversal)\n- [OWASP Unvalidated Redirects and Forwards Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Unvalidated_Redirects_and_Forwards_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-22: Improper Limitation of a Pathname to a Restricted Directory ('Path Traversal')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/22.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-22",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/22.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-22"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a71db1e8-e74f-3325-89e6-91e607a4edd3",
              "name": "Path Traversal Vulnerability due to Inadequate Input Validation and Path Canonicalization",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nPath Traversal Vulnerability due to Inadequate Input Validation and Path Canonicalization is a common security issue in JavaScript applications. This vulnerability occurs when an application uses user-controllable data to construct a pathname that is intended to identify a file or directory located underneath a restricted parent directory, but the data is not properly validated or sanitized, allowing for directory traversal characters to be injected.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the application is resolving a file path using a user-controlled variable (`file`). If an attacker can control the value of `file`, they could potentially access sensitive files outside of the intended 'logs/' directory by using path traversal characters (e.g., `../../etc/passwd`).\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. Avoid using user input to construct file paths directly.\n2. If it's necessary to use user input in file paths, validate the input strictly. For example, only allow alphanumeric characters, and reject inputs with special characters like \"/\", \"\\\", \"..\", and so on.\n3. Use a library or framework function that generates the absolute path and validates it to be under the expected base directory.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```javascript\nvar express = require('express');\nvar path = require('path');\nvar app = express();\n\napp.get('/download/:file(*)', function(req, res, next){\n  var file = req.params.file;\n  var filePath = path.join(__dirname, 'logs', file);\n  res.download(filePath, function (err) {\n    if (!err) return; // File was sent successfully\n    if (err && err.status !== 404) return next(err); // Non-404 error\n    // File for download not found\n    res.statusCode = 404;\n    res.send('Cant find that file, sorry!');\n  });\n});\n```\n\nIn this fix, we use the `path.join()` function to construct the file path, and the `res.download()` function to send the file. This ensures that the file path is always within the 'logs/' directory, and prevents path traversal attacks.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- express\n- path\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Path Traversal](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Path_Traversal)\n- [CWE-22: Improper Limitation of a Pathname to a Restricted Directory ('Path Traversal')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/22.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-22",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/22.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-22"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "94ea3c47-470f-392d-a52c-18788562d321",
              "name": "\"Cryptographic Weakness in Crypto.pseudoRandomBytes()/Math.random() Random Number Generator\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `Math.random()` function in JavaScript is a pseudo-random number generator, which is not suitable for cryptographic purposes. The numbers generated by this function are predictable, which makes it possible for an attacker to guess the output and compromise the security of the application. This is a known cryptographic weakness in JavaScript.\n\nThe `Crypto.pseudoRandomBytes()` function is a part of the `crypto` module in Node.js, which is also not suitable for generating cryptographically secure random numbers. This function is deprecated and should not be used in new code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use a cryptographically secure pseudo-random number generator (CSPRNG) instead of `Math.random()` or `Crypto.pseudoRandomBytes()`. In Node.js, you can use the `crypto.randomInt()` function to generate a secure random number.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```javascript\nconst crypto = require('crypto');\n\nproduct.price = product.price ?? crypto.randomInt(1, 10);\n```\n\nThis code uses the `crypto.randomInt()` function to generate a secure random number between 1 and 10 (inclusive).\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe fixed code requires the `crypto` module, which is a built-in module in Node.js. No additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Insecure Randomness](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Insecure_Randomness)\n- [CWE-330: Use of Insufficiently Random Values](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/330.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-330",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/330.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-330"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "101e7eed-3c20-3b7a-81da-adf6e91de366",
              "name": "Path Traversal Vulnerability due to Inadequate Input Validation and Path Canonicalization",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nPath Traversal Vulnerability due to Inadequate Input Validation and Path Canonicalization is a common vulnerability in web applications. It occurs when an application uses user-controllable data to access or open files on the server. If the user input is not properly validated, an attacker can manipulate the input to traverse outside of the intended directory and gain unauthorized access to files and directories.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the `res.sendFile()` function is used to send a file from the server to the client. The file path is constructed using the `path.resolve()` function with a base directory of 'ftp/quarantine/' and a filename provided by the user (`file`). If the `file` variable is not properly validated, an attacker could provide a path traversal string (e.g., '../../etc/passwd') to access files outside of the intended directory.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. Avoid using user input to construct file paths directly.\n2. Validate user input strictly. For example, you can use a whitelist of allowed characters in the filename, and reject any input that contains characters not in the whitelist.\n3. Use a library or built-in function to canonicalize the file path, which resolves any path traversal sequences ('../' or './') in the input.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the provided code using the `path.basename()` function to ensure that the user input is a filename, not a path:\n\n```javascript\nvar express = require('express');\nvar path = require('path');\nvar app = express();\n\napp.get('/download', function(req, res){\n  var file = req.query.file;\n  var filename = path.basename(file);\n  res.sendFile(path.resolve('ftp/quarantine/', filename));\n});\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following libraries:\n\n- `express`: A web application framework for Node.js.\n- `path`: A built-in Node.js module for handling and transforming file paths.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Path Traversal](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Path_Traversal)\n- [CWE-22: Improper Limitation of a Pathname to a Restricted Directory ('Path Traversal')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/22.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-22",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/22.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-22"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "8ab780ed-425d-368c-9f08-8be352310b3c",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA potential Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability can occur when user-controlled data is used in an HTML string. This can allow an attacker to inject malicious scripts into web pages viewed by other users. In the provided JavaScript code, the error message is being logged using a template literal that includes a function call to `utils.getErrorMessage(err)`. If the `err` object is user-controlled and not properly sanitized, it could lead to an XSS vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always escape user-controlled data before including it in an HTML string. This can be done using various methods, such as using the `encodeURIComponent()` function in JavaScript, or using a library that provides HTML escaping functions.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nlogger.error(`Could not create memory: ${encodeURIComponent(utils.getErrorMessage(err))}`);\n```\n\nIn this fix, the `encodeURIComponent()` function is used to escape any potentially harmful characters in the error message.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code does not specify any library dependencies. However, it is assumed that a `logger` object and a `utils` object with a `getErrorMessage` function are available in the scope where this code is executed.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1ce04c2e-0f31-3cb5-809a-f8c42aedc863",
              "name": "\"Hardcoded Username Vulnerability: Store in Environment Variable\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nHardcoded Username Vulnerability: Store in Environment Variable is a security flaw that occurs when a developer hardcodes sensitive information, such as usernames, into the application's source code. This can lead to unauthorized access if the source code is exposed or if the hardcoded information is discovered.\n\nIn JavaScript, this vulnerability can occur when a username is hardcoded into the application's memory, as shown in the following code:\n\n```javascript\nmemory.user = 'emma'\n```\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid hardcoding sensitive information directly into your application's source code. Instead, use environment variables to store this information. Environment variables are a type of dynamic-named value that can affect the way running processes behave on a computer. They are part of the environment in which a process runs.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the vulnerability in the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nmemory.user = process.env.USERNAME\n```\n\nIn this code, `process.env.USERNAME` is an environment variable that stores the username. This way, the username is not hardcoded into the application's source code.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code does not require any library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top 10 2017 Category A2: Broken Authentication](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A2_2017-Broken_Authentication)\n- [CWE-798: Use of Hard-coded Credentials](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-798",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-798"
                ]
              }
