            {
              "id": "844410fd-334d-3b48-a0a0-e1a42215b686",
              "name": "Potential SQL Injection Vulnerability Detected in Sequelize Statement",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe code snippet provided is vulnerable to SQL Injection, a common security vulnerability that allows an attacker to manipulate SQL queries. This vulnerability is present in the Sequelize statement where user-supplied input (`criteria`) is directly embedded into the SQL query without proper sanitization or parameterization. This could allow an attacker to inject malicious SQL code, leading to unauthorized data access, data corruption, or even data loss.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid directly concatenating user-supplied input into SQL queries. Instead, use parameterized queries or prepared statements, which can ensure that user input is always treated as literal data, not part of the SQL command.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer version of the code using Sequelize's replacements feature for parameterized queries:\n\n```javascript\nmodels.sequelize.query(\n  `SELECT * FROM Products WHERE ((name LIKE :searchName OR description LIKE :searchDesc) AND deletedAt IS NULL) ORDER BY name`,\n  {\n    replacements: { searchName: `%${criteria}%`, searchDesc: `%${criteria}%` },\n    type: models.sequelize.QueryTypes.SELECT\n  }\n);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Sequelize: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite, and Microsoft SQL Server.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-89: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an SQL Command ('SQL Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-89",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-89"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b97a4123-4625-348c-b764-6da1b1d667d7",
              "name": "Unvalidated User-Supplied URL Redirection Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnvalidated User-Supplied URL Redirection Vulnerability, also known as Open Redirect, is a type of security vulnerability that occurs when an application incorporates user-supplied data into a URL redirect without validating it. This allows attackers to redirect victims to phishing or malware sites, or use forwards to access unauthorized pages.\n\nIn JavaScript, this vulnerability can occur when using the `res.redirect()` function, as in the provided sink:\n\n```javascript\nres.redirect(toUrl);\n```\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate user-supplied input before using it in a URL redirect. This can be done by:\n\n- Whitelisting known good URLs.\n- Checking if the supplied URL belongs to the allowed domain.\n- Avoiding the use of user-supplied data in redirect operations whenever possible.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the provided sink using URL parsing and validation:\n\n```javascript\nvar url = require('url');\nvar allowedHost = 'www.example.com';\n\n// Parse the user-supplied URL\nvar userUrl = url.parse(toUrl);\n\n// Check if the host of the user-supplied URL matches the allowed host\nif (userUrl.host === allowedHost) {\n  res.redirect(toUrl);\n} else {\n  res.redirect('/error');\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe above code example requires the built-in `url` module in Node.js.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Unvalidated Redirects and Forwards Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Unvalidated_Redirects_and_Forwards_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-601: URL Redirection to Untrusted Site ('Open Redirect')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/601.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-601",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/601.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-601"
                ]
              }
