            {
              "id": "78f2696d-2424-3cc5-b90e-30bdffb38226",
              "name": "Potential XSS vulnerability due to undefined 'subs' used with '<script>' tag.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe code snippet provided is vulnerable to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks due to the use of an undefined variable `subs` in a `<script>` tag. XSS vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. In this case, if the `subs` variable contains malicious script, it will be executed when the page is loaded.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate and sanitize user input. Never insert untrusted data directly into your HTML or JavaScript code. Instead, use safe APIs or context-specific output encoding. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA possible fix for this vulnerability would be to sanitize the `subs` variable before using it in the `<script>` tag. Here's an example using the DOMPurify library:\n\n```javascript\nvar DOMPurify = require('dompurify');\nvar sanitizedSubs = DOMPurify.sanitize(subs);\ncompiledTemplate = compiledTemplate.replace('<script id=\"subtitle\"></script>', '<script id=\"subtitle\" type=\"text/vtt\" data-label=\"English\" data-lang=\"en\">' + sanitizedSubs + '</script>')\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the DOMPurify library to sanitize the `subs` variable.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "0dc1df90-b997-3bc5-bb61-a3212ead9635",
              "name": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability in JavaScript occurs when a promise is rejected, but there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including crashes, and can potentially expose sensitive information in error messages.\n\nIn the provided code, the vulnerability arises from the fact that the promise returned by `ProductModel.create()` is not properly handled. If the promise is rejected, the error is logged, but the execution continues to the `then()` block. If `persistedProduct` is null (which it will be in case of a rejection), an error is thrown, but this error is not caught anywhere, leading to an Unhandled Promise Rejection.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always have error handling mechanisms in place for promises. This can be done by chaining a `.catch()` block after your `.then()` block, or by using `try/catch` with `async/await`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code:\n\n```javascript\ntry {\n  const persistedProduct = await ProductModel.create({\n    name: product.name,\n    description: product.description,\n    price: product.price,\n    deluxePrice: product.deluxePrice ?? product.price,\n    image: product.image\n  })\n\n  if (persistedProduct != null) {\n    if (useForChristmasSpecialChallenge) { datacache.products.christmasSpecial = persistedProduct }\n    if (urlForProductTamperingChallenge) {\n      datacache.products.osaft = persistedProduct\n      await datacache.challenges.changeProductChallenge.update({\n        description: customizeChangeProductChallenge(\n          datacache.challenges.changeProductChallenge.description,\n          config.get('challenges.overwriteUrlForProductTamperingChallenge'),\n          persistedProduct)\n      })\n    }\n    if (deletedDate) void deleteProduct(persistedProduct.id) // TODO Rename into \"isDeleted\" or \"deletedFlag\" in config for v14.x release\n  } else {\n    throw new Error('No persisted product found!')\n  }\n  return persistedProduct\n} catch (err) {\n  logger.error(`Could not insert Product ${product.name}: ${utils.getErrorMessage(err)}`)\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- A database ORM library that provides the `ProductModel.create()` function. This could be Sequelize, Mongoose, or a similar library.\n- A logging library for the `logger.error()` function.\n- A utility library for the `utils.getErrorMessage()` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-754",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-754"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "e011127d-18c1-31c4-89bd-d4b48baf606a",
              "name": "Potential XSS vulnerability due to undefined 'subs' used with '<script>' tag.",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe code snippet provided is vulnerable to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks due to the use of an undefined variable `subs` in conjunction with a `<script>` tag. XSS attacks occur when an attacker is able to inject malicious scripts into web pages viewed by other users. In this case, the `subs` variable could potentially contain user input, which is then checked for a specific XSS payload (`</script><script>alert(`xss`)</script>`). If the `subs` variable contains this payload, it would be executed by the browser, leading to an XSS attack.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. **Validate Input:** Ensure that all user inputs are validated before they are processed. This includes checking for the correct data type, length, format, and range.\n\n2. **Encode Output:** Encode the output to ensure that characters are treated as data, not as code. This can prevent an attacker from injecting malicious scripts.\n\n3. **Use Content Security Policy (CSP):** Implement a Content Security Policy (CSP) to restrict the sources from which scripts can be loaded. This can prevent the execution of malicious scripts.\n\n4. **Use HttpOnly Cookies:** Use HttpOnly cookies to prevent scripts from accessing sensitive data stored in cookies.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe code should be updated to validate and sanitize the `subs` variable before it is used:\n\n```javascript\nconst sanitize = require('sanitize-html');\n\nchallengeUtils.solveIf(challenges.videoXssChallenge, () => { \n    let sanitizedSubs = sanitize(subs);\n    return utils.contains(sanitizedSubs, '</script><script>alert(`xss`)</script>') \n})\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `sanitize-html`: This library is used to sanitize the `subs` variable to prevent XSS attacks.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE-79): Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b5bf9c81-a679-3a43-8d3a-3798c1b651ff",
              "name": "Non-literal variable string concatenation vulnerability in util.format/console.log function",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn JavaScript, the `console.error` function is used to log error messages to the console. However, when used with non-literal variable string concatenation, it can lead to a vulnerability. This vulnerability occurs when user-controlled data is concatenated with a string and then passed to the `console.error` function. An attacker can potentially manipulate the data to cause unexpected behavior in the application.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid directly concatenating user-controlled data with strings that are passed to the `console.error` function. Instead, use parameterized messages or string formatting functions that do not evaluate the string as code.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of concatenating the string, you can pass the variables as separate arguments to the `console.error` function. This way, the variables are not evaluated as code.\n\n```javascript\nconsole.error('Error in timed startup function: %s', name, err);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `console.error` function is a built-in function in JavaScript and does not require any library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Unvalidated Redirects and Forwards Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Unvalidated_Redirects_and_Forwards_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-117: Improper Output Neutralization for Logs](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/117.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-117",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/117.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-117"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "bfbed3ec-722c-3da9-adf5-4154b2d4ce21",
              "name": "Enabled Directory Listing/Indexing Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Enabled Directory Listing/Indexing Vulnerability\" in JavaScript is a security flaw that allows unauthorized users to view the contents of directories that are not explicitly forbidden. This can lead to information disclosure as the attacker can view sensitive files or data. In the provided code, the `serveIndex` middleware is used to serve directory listings, which can lead to this vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should disable directory listing unless it is absolutely necessary. If directory listing is required, ensure that it is only enabled for directories that do not contain sensitive information. Also, ensure that access to these directories is properly authenticated and authorized.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nTo fix the vulnerability in the provided code, you can remove the `serveIndex` middleware for the `/.well-known` route:\n\n```javascript\napp.use('/.well-known', serveIndexMiddleware)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Express.js: A web application framework for Node.js.\n- serve-index: Middleware for serving directory listings.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-548: Information Exposure Through Directory Listing](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/548.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-548",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/548.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-548"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "9035dc8a-f25c-327b-a544-5c2e899dfeec",
              "name": "XSS Vulnerability in User-Controlled HTML Data",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the application is logging an error message that includes user-supplied data (`text`). If this data is not properly sanitized, it could lead to an XSS vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate XSS vulnerabilities, you should:\n\n- Use frameworks that automatically escape XSS by design, such as Ruby on Rails, React JS, etc.\n- Escape untrusted HTTP request data based on the context in the HTML output (body, attribute, JavaScript, CSS, or URL).\n- Apply context-sensitive encoding when modifying the browser document on the client side.\n- Validate, filter, or sanitize user input.\n- Use Content Security Policy (CSP) as a defense-in-depth mitigating control against XSS.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code snippet, you should ensure that the `text` variable is properly sanitized before it is included in the error message. This can be done using a library like DOMPurify.\n\n```javascript\nconst DOMPurify = require('dompurify');\nconst { JSDOM } = require('jsdom');\n\nconst window = new JSDOM('').window;\nconst purify = DOMPurify(window);\n\nlet sanitizedText = purify.sanitize(text);\nlogger.error(`Could not insert Product Review ${sanitizedText}: ${utils.getErrorMessage(err)}`);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- DOMPurify\n- jsdom\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "bff279aa-8813-3ae9-87e3-1e649af40350",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA potential Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability from user-controlled data in HTML string is a type of security vulnerability that allows an attacker to inject malicious scripts into web pages viewed by other users. This vulnerability can occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the logger.error function is vulnerable to XSS attacks because it directly includes user-controlled data (`product.name` and `utils.getErrorMessage(err)`) in the output without any sanitization or encoding.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always escape untrusted data based on the HTML context (body, attribute, JavaScript, CSS, or URL) that the data will be placed into. In this case, you should escape the `product.name` and `utils.getErrorMessage(err)` before including them in the logger.error function.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```javascript\nlet productName = escapeHTML(product.name);\nlet errorMessage = escapeHTML(utils.getErrorMessage(err));\nlogger.error(`Could not insert Product ${productName}: ${errorMessage}`);\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, the `escapeHTML` function is used to escape any potentially harmful characters in the `product.name` and `utils.getErrorMessage(err)`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code does not specify any library dependencies. However, to implement the `escapeHTML` function, you might need a library like lodash's `_.escape` function.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
