              "id": "ffa4937f-d303-3c68-8f4c-a0e0b99af547",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA potential Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability from user-controlled data in HTML string exists when an application includes untrusted data in a new HTML string used in a web page. XSS vulnerabilities occur when an application includes user-controllable data in a page sent to the user without properly validating or escaping that content.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the `basketItem.BasketId` and `utils.getErrorMessage(err)` are user-controlled data that are directly included in the HTML string without any sanitization or encoding. This could potentially lead to an XSS attack if an attacker can control the `BasketId` or the error message.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. Always validate user-controlled data to ensure it conforms to the expected format.\n2. Use appropriate context-dependent output encoding when inserting user-controlled data into HTML string.\n3. Use modern frameworks that automatically escape XSS by design, such as React JS.\n4. Use Content Security Policy (CSP) to reduce the severity of any XSS vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nYou should encode the user-controlled data before including it in the HTML string. Here is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```javascript\nlogger.error(`Could not insert BasketItem for BasketId ${encodeForHTML(basketItem.BasketId)}: ${encodeForHTML(utils.getErrorMessage(err))}`)\n```\n\nIn this code, `encodeForHTML` is a function that properly encodes user-controlled data for use in HTML context. You need to implement this function or use a library that provides this functionality.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example does not provide enough context to determine the required library dependencies.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ca565583-1fb2-3cf3-ae61-5bb7e73e7ac1",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA potential Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability can occur when user-controlled data is used in an HTML string without proper sanitization or encoding. In the provided JavaScript code, the `basket.UserId` and `utils.getErrorMessage(err)` are user-controlled data that are directly inserted into an HTML string. If an attacker can control these values, they can inject malicious scripts that can be executed in the browser of any user viewing the generated HTML.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always encode user-controlled data before inserting it into an HTML string. This can be done using built-in JavaScript functions or third-party libraries that are designed for this purpose. Additionally, you should use context-specific encoding when inserting data into different parts of an HTML document.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```javascript\nconst sanitizeHtml = require('sanitize-html');\n\nlet userId = sanitizeHtml(basket.UserId);\nlet errorMessage = sanitizeHtml(utils.getErrorMessage(err));\n\nlogger.error(`Could not insert Basket for UserId ${userId}: ${errorMessage}`);\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, we use the `sanitize-html` library to sanitize the user-controlled data before inserting it into the HTML string. This will remove any potentially harmful scripts from the data.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n- sanitize-html\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
