            {
              "id": "aeb1f655-66da-3faa-b38a-ede19a057b6b",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA potential Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability can occur when user-controlled data is directly included in an HTML string. In this case, the user-controlled data is `authoredComment` and `UserId`. If an attacker can control these values, they can inject malicious scripts that will be executed in the browser of any user viewing the generated HTML.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always escape user-controlled data before including it in an HTML string. This can be done using a variety of methods, including using a library designed for this purpose, or by using built-in JavaScript functions like `encodeURIComponent()` or `encodeURI()`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix using `encodeURIComponent()`:\n\n```javascript\nlogger.error(`Could not insert Feedback ${encodeURIComponent(authoredComment)} mapped to UserId ${encodeURIComponent(UserId)}: ${utils.getErrorMessage(err)}`)\n```\n\nThis will ensure that any special characters in `authoredComment` and `UserId` are properly escaped, preventing them from being interpreted as part of the HTML.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example does not appear to require any specific library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
