            {
              "id": "2edfe910-78ee-3c0e-80bd-20383f61c559",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when data enters a program from an untrusted source and is used to generate dynamic HTML content. The potential XSS vulnerability from user-controlled data in an HTML string arises when an application includes untrusted data in a new HTML string used to generate a page. An attacker can use this vulnerability to inject malicious scripts into the web page viewed by other users. These scripts can steal sensitive data from users, perform actions on their behalf, or launch other attacks against the system.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the potential XSS vulnerability exists because the `logger.error` function uses template literals to include untrusted data (`answer` and `UserId`) directly into the log message. If an attacker can control the `answer` or `UserId` values, they could potentially inject malicious scripts.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. Avoid including untrusted data directly into your HTML. Instead, use safe APIs that automatically handle the encoding for you.\n2. Use context-specific output encoding/escaping before including untrusted data in HTML output.\n3. Validate and sanitize user input to ensure it conforms to the expected format.\n4. Implement a Content Security Policy (CSP) to reduce the severity of any XSS vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix using the `escape-html` library to escape any potentially harmful characters:\n\n```javascript\nconst escapeHtml = require('escape-html');\n\nlogger.error(`Could not insert SecurityAnswer ${escapeHtml(answer)} mapped to UserId ${escapeHtml(UserId)}: ${utils.getErrorMessage(err)}`);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `escape-html` library to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
