            {
              "id": "a6e7a2a5-722e-37e8-81bf-c68a982a8e33",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when data enters a program from an untrusted source and is used to generate dynamic HTML content. The \"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\" vulnerability in JavaScript refers to the situation where user-controlled data is directly included in an HTML string without proper sanitization or encoding. This can allow an attacker to inject malicious scripts into the web page, which can then be executed in the context of the victim's browser.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `orderId` and the error message from `utils.getErrorMessage(err)` are directly included in the logged error message. If these values are controlled by the user and contain malicious scripts, they can lead to an XSS vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always sanitize and/or encode user-controlled data before including it in an HTML string. Sanitization involves removing or replacing any potentially harmful characters in the input, while encoding involves converting these characters into a safe format that can be included in HTML without risk.\n\nIn addition, you should use context-specific output encoding libraries to ensure that the data is correctly encoded for the specific HTML context in which it is used. For example, data included in an HTML attribute should be encoded differently than data included in a script block.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```javascript\nconst sanitize = require('sanitize-html');\n\nlet sanitizedOrderId = sanitize(orderId);\nlet sanitizedErrorMessage = sanitize(utils.getErrorMessage(err));\n\nlogger.error(`Could not insert Order ${sanitizedOrderId}: ${sanitizedErrorMessage}`);\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, the `sanitize-html` library is used to sanitize the `orderId` and the error message before they are included in the logged error message.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n- sanitize-html\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS (Cross Site Scripting) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
